# Agent Instructions

This project is a .NET gateway integration built on the Intropy framework: a
resident HTTP service that accepts orders pushed to `POST {{ .endpointPath }}`, runs
each request body through the Intropy extractor pipeline, and publishes the
result as a CloudEvent to the system's pub/sub. The caller is the retry
mechanism — the status code tells it whether to resend.

## Project overview

- Framework: .NET 10, ASP.NET Core
- App type: Resident web service, hosted by `Intropy.Framework.Components`
  (`ComponentHost.RunAsync<{{ .componentClass }}>`). The framework wires the web host,
  the endpoint, `/healthz` and `/readyz`, telemetry, the Dapr client, the
  platform-service clients and the publisher.
- Identity: the `Manifest` property of `{{ .componentClass }}`. The framework reads the
  component name (`{{ .componentName }}`), the organization (`{{ .organization }}`), the
  message and the event source from it, and checks the declared endpoint against its `Port`
  (`{{ .port }}`) and `EndpointPath` (`{{ .endpointPath }}`). The payload is named where it
  is published (`Publishes<{{ .payloadType }}>(...)`), the same type on every endpoint. The same
  identity is recorded in `.intropy/scaffold.json` (written by `int create`, read by
  `intropy sys create`); changing it means editing the manifest and the record's matching
  `values` keys together.
- App id: `{{ .appId }}`
- Endpoint: `{{ .port }}` at `POST {{ .endpointPath }}`, on HTTP port 8080 (ASP.NET Core
  `http_ports` / `urls` override it). The system host serves it locally on
  `http://localhost:{{ .localPort }}`, from `src/Properties/launchSettings.json`. The
  manifest's `Port` and `EndpointPath` declare the endpoint the system names for the system
  host and the ingress; the port is stamped on every published event and scopes the
  idempotency records.
- Message: `{{ .publishes }}` — what the gateway publishes; the message name is the
  CloudEvents type.
- Channel: `{{ .channel }}` at scaffold time. The code takes the
  pub/sub and topic from `INTROPY_PUBSUB_NAME` and `INTROPY_TOPIC_NAME`, which the
  system host and the deployment set; without them startup fails.
- CloudEvent envelope: source `{{ .eventSourceValue }}`, type `{{ .publishes }}`.
- Responses: `202` published (`{ requestId, eventId }`), `200` duplicate, `422`
  rejected (routed as a business incident), `413` body too large, `503` +
  `Retry-After` when the gateway cannot take the item now, `500` when a
  technical failure would recur on a resend. Every response
  carries `X-Request-Id` (the caller's, or one assigned).
- Idempotency Service: Dapr app id `{{ .idempotencyAppId }}`; Business Incident
  Service: `{{ .businessIncidentsAppId }}` (`src/appsettings.json`, `PlatformServices:*`).
- Shared contracts: the published payload type (`{{ .payloadType }}`) lives in the
  sibling project `../{{ .contracts }}`. `{{ .payloadType }}Request` — the body callers
  send — stays local in `src/Model/`.

## Important files

- `src/{{ .componentClass }}.cs` - the component: what it accepts on its endpoint and what it
  publishes (`Inputs`: `Accepts<{{ .payloadType }}Request>()` then `Publishes<{{ .payloadType }}>(...)`),
  with its pipeline's idempotency
  rule ({{ if .empty }}throwing lambdas to implement{{ else }}`OrderId`/`OrderDate`{{ end }}) and output event rule (subject and time).
  Incidents take the framework's default name: the business id the `Deserializer`
  sets in `BusinessIncidentContextKeys.BusinessId`, else the request id
  (`request:<id>`).
- `src/Program.cs` - entrypoint: `ComponentHost.RunAsync<{{ .componentClass }}>(args)`.
- `src/Process/` - the pipeline's steps: `Deserializer`, `Validator`,
  `Transformer`, named in `Steps(steps => steps.Deserialize<...>().Validate<...>().Transform<...>())`
  on the endpoint's pipeline, in the order they run; enrichment steps go between `Validate` and
  `Transform` as `.Extract<T>()`.
- `src/Model/{{ .payloadType }}Request.cs` - the request body's shape.
- `src/appsettings.json` - platform-service app ids and the `Gateway` options
  (`MaxRequestBodyBytes`, `RetryAfter`).
- `src/{{ .name }}.http` - {{ if .empty }}a published, a not-JSON and the probe requests{{ else }}one sample request per response{{ end }}, for a running
  gateway; `src/http-client.env.json` holds the host per environment.
- `src/Properties/launchSettings.json` - the local port ({{ .localPort }}) the system
  host serves the gateway on; `local` in `src/http-client.env.json` must match it.
- `Dockerfile` - multi-stage build on the chiseled ASP.NET Core runtime
  (`docker build --build-context contracts=../{{ .contracts }} -t {{ .appId }} .`).
- `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Unit/` - pipeline step unit tests (`task test:unit`).
- `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/` - the component's rules end to end (the
  event it publishes, the idempotency key it records, the incident a rejected
  order raises) and its startup composition, against `GatewayTestHost`, which
  serves the production graph in memory with the topic and the platform clients
  faked (`task test:integration`). The HTTP contract itself is the framework's,
  and is not retested here.

## Build and test

Components do not run standalone — this gateway runs via its system host, which
provides every Dapr component (pub/sub, platform services) and the
`INTROPY_*` environment. The component-level loop is build and test:

```bash
task build
task test
```

## Development notes

- A second endpoint carrying other data — another source system's format of the
  order — is a second `.On(port, path).Accepts<TInput>().Publishes<{{ .payloadType }}>(...)`
  in `{{ .componentClass }}.Inputs`, with its own input model and steps; every
  endpoint publishes `{{ .payloadType }}`. The manifest names only the first
  endpoint, so add the second to the system host by hand.
- Steps that share data beyond the context's metadata (a looked-up customer, say) get a
  context of the component's own: a record deriving from `Context`, created with
  `.Accepts<{{ .payloadType }}Request>().WithContext((metadata, isRetry) => new MyContext(metadata, isRetry))`.
  Every step of that pipeline is then typed on it (`DeserializeStep<{{ .payloadType }}Request, MyContext>`, ...).
- Authentication is not in the component: the environment's ingress owns it.
  Override `ConfigureEndpoints` (e.g. `RequireAuthorization()`) and
  `ConfigureApplication` in `{{ .componentClass }}` to add it in the app.
- An infrastructure failure (sidecar, pub/sub, idempotency or incident service
  down) answers `503`; the caller resends. Any other technical failure (a step
  throwing in `Transformer`, an output event without a subject) answers `500`
  and is not resent: it needs a fix in the gateway. A business failure answers
  `422` and is not worth resending unchanged.
{{- if .empty }}
- The step bodies are scaffolded empty (`NotImplementedException`), and
  `{{ .payloadType }}Request` is an empty record. The idempotency and output-event
  rules stay in `{{ .componentClass }}.Inputs` with throwing lambdas: point them at a
  stable business identifier and business timestamp — a per-delivery value
  (fresh GUID, wall-clock time) lets every duplicate through.
- The test suite is real and fails out of the box by design — `task test` is
  red until the steps and the lambdas land. Until then every request answers
  `422`, since the throwing `Deserializer` is routed as a business incident. Only
  the composition test starts green. The full sample's rule tests (event subject
  and time, idempotency key and version, a rejected order's incident) are
  deliberately not scaffolded, since their values are the implementation's to
  define. Add them as the rules land.
{{- end }}

